## 1  Introduction
Severe electromagnetic pollution caused by the extensive utilization of electronics and communication devices has created global issues, adversely affecting the public health and environment, as well as causing interference during the operation of precise instruments and apparatus [1]. Therefore, to overcome these concerning issues relate to electromagnetic irradiation, considerable efforts have been devoted to the development of efficient electromagnetic absorbing materials with strong absorption performances, wide bandwidths and other suitable characteristics, such as a less weight and thin thickness [2–5]. Generally, an electromagnetic absorber absorbs the incident electromagnetic waves by effectively attenuating and converting the electromagnetic energy into thermal or other forms of energy to control the secondary reflected radiation [6]. Primarily, the absorption effect is induced by the dielectric and magnetic losses mechanisms [7]. Accordingly, materials exhibiting dielectric loss (e.g., carbon materials and conductive polymers) have been widely explored owing to their low density, high carrier mobility, and facile manipulation of properties for ideal absorption [8]. Similarly, materials exhibiting magnetic loss (e.g., magnetic metals, ferrites, and metal oxides) have garnered considerable research attention owing to their synergistic absorbing ability and broadband performance [9, 10].
Despite the abovementioned merits, an immoderate dielectric constant or conductivity of the dielectric loss materials causes impedance mismatch, which results in a low absorption performance [11]. Moreover, materials exhibiting magnetic loss are generally not lightweight owing to their high density and readily undergo corrosion with low thermal stability, which restrict the practical applications of the corresponding electromagnetic absorbers [12]. Therefore, the integration of the loss mechanisms is essential for complementing the insufficient single‐loss mechanism and improve the impedance matching properties [13]. For instance, Yan et al. [14] prepared $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hollow nanoparticles grown on graphene sheets using a mild solvothermal method, which produced a minimum reflection loss of $-50.2~\mathrm{dB}$ at $12.1\ \mathrm{GHz}$ and an effective absorption bandwidth of $3.2\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $2.0\mathrm{mm}$ . Wang et al. [15] manufactured $\mathrm{CNT/BaFe}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{19}$ composites by introducing carbon nanotubes (CNTs) during the autoignition of a precursor of ${\bf B a F e}_{12}{\bf O}_{19}$ . Owing to the improved dispersibility of CNTs, the reflection loss was − 43.9 dB at $4.3\:\mathrm{GHz}$ , and the effective bandwidth increased up to $3.9\mathrm{GHz}$ for a CNT loading of only $2\mathrm{wt}\%$ . Several reports have emphasized the use of innovative architecture, such as hollow [16], core–shell [17, 18], two‐dimensional [19], urchin‐like [20], and hierarchical foam [21] structures, to enhance the relaxation loss by utilizing the characteristic of a large specific surface area with numerous interfaces and achieve efficient electromagnetic absorbers.
In recent years, metal–organic framework (MOF) derivatives have been considered novel and desirable candidates for electromagnetic wave absorption, owing to their distinctive characteristics, such as a large specific surface area, an adjustable composition, tunable morphology, and high phase uniformity, which comprise diverse metal nodes coordinated with organic linkers in appropriate solvents [22]. Moreover, MOFs have garnered considerable research attention owing to the contemporaneous encompassment of uniformly distributed dielectric and magnetic materials as well as alterable modification of phase for synthesizing the metal oxide‐ and magnetic carbon‐based materials by regulating the heat‐treatment process [23]. Zhang et al. [24] fabricated MOF‐derived $\mathrm{{ZrO}}_{2}/\mathrm{{C}}$ from UIO‐66 through solvothermal synthesis and pyrolysis. Based on this research, the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were uniformly distributed in the carbon matrix and demonstrated an adequate absorption performance with a minimum reflection loss of − 58.7 dB at $16.8~\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thickness of $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ and an effective absorption bandwidth of $5.5\:\mathrm{GHz}$ . Qin et al. [25] reported multi‐layered flower‐like $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ graphite nanosheets obtained via calcination of ZIF‐67/graphite nanosheet precursor. They reported that the novel microstructure is beneficial for promoting the dielectric and magnetic losses, achieving a reflection loss of − 52.46 dB at $7.36\:\mathrm{GHz}$ and a corresponding absorption bandwidth of $3.52\:\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of $3.4~\mathrm{mm}$ . However, heterogeneous materials, such as graphene and carbon fibers, have been introduced to reinforce the dielectric polarization for high‐performance absorbers displaying inadequate dispersibility [4, 26]. Besides, suitable electromagnetic properties, such as complex permittivity and permeability, for optimal impedance matching are unpredictable because of the involved thermal treatment under varying temperature conditions [27]. Moreover, a complicated two‐step synthesis is required to fabricate the bimetallic metal system from MOF and control the permittivity to improve the dielectric loss and impedance matching [28]. Also, a conductive carbon coated bimetallic MOF derivative showed a sharp downward reduction of permeability in high frequency range, representing $\upmu^{\prime}<1.0$ over $10\mathrm{GHz}$ [29].
In this study, we successfully prepared hexagonal $\mathrm{Fe}/\$ Co bimetallic MOF derivatives via the calcination and pyrolysis of the MIL‐88A‐structured FeCo‐MOF, wherein Fe was partially substituted with Co, via one‐pot synthesis for $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ , respectively [30]. Among various magnetic metals, the $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ bimetallic system exhibits improved magnetic properties, such as saturation magnetization and a corresponding relative permeability in GHz‐ scale frequency, that intensifies the magnetic loss and yields promising electromagnetic absorbing performance [31]. These uniform‐particle‐sized derivates can be successfully controlled according to the rule of mixture to fabricate the hybridized absorber in contrast to coalescence and agglomeration, demonstrating the synergistic dielectric and magnetic losses in the high frequency range contributing to outstanding electromagnetic absorption performance. Significantly, the absorber can exhibit a minimum reflection of $\cdot-52.29{\mathrm{~dB}}$ at $1.9~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness and a broad effective bandwidth of $7.23\mathrm{GHz}$ at $0.9\mathrm{mm}$ thickness. Moreover, this research establishes the foundations for a feasible combination of high performance and wide effective bandwidth in the field of electromagnetic wave absorption applications.
## 2  Experimental
## 2.1  Materials
Iron (III) nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $98\%$ ) was purchased from Alfa Aesar (MA, USA). Cobalt (II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ $98\%$ ) and fumaric acid $(98\%)$
were purchased from Sigma‐Aldrich (MO, USA). N,N‐ dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ ) and ethanol were procured from Samchun Chemical (Seoul, Korea). All chemicals were used as received without any further purification. Thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU, A‐9401), obtained from Songwon Industrial Co., Ltd. (Ulsan, Korea), was dissolved in DMF and methyl ethyl ketone (MEK) solvents at a concentration of $30~\mathrm{wt}\%$ to serve as a polymer matrix in the fabrication of the MOF derivative composites, the electromagnetic properties of which were subsequently analyzed.
## 2.2  Synthesis of the FeCo‐MOF and fabrication of MOF derivatives
The FeCo‐MOF was synthesized following the Fe‐MIL‐ 88A synthesis method with slight modification, as reported earlier [30]. First, $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(96.0\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.238\ \mathrm{mol})$ and $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $(69.2~\mathrm{g},0.238~\mathrm{mol})$ were dissolved in $500~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and mixed with fumaric acid $(504~\mathrm{g}$ , $4.34\mathrm{mol};$ ) dissolved in $2000\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The mixtures were placed in a heating mantle at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ . Thereafter, the resulting precipitations were isolated via centrifugation and washed with ethanol and dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ . Subsequently, $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was obtained by calcinating the of FeCo‐ MOF in air at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{inin}$ , and FeCo embedded in the porous carbon as $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ was obtained via pyrolysis of the FeCo‐MOF in Ar at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{Cmin}$ .
## 2.3  Preparation of MOF derivative composites
FeCo‐MOF derivative composites were prepared via a hot‐pressing process. First, FeCo‐MOF derivatives were mechanically mixed with TPU at a weight ratio of 70:30 using a planetary centrifugal mixer (ARE‐310, Thinky, Japan) for $3\ \mathrm{min}$ at 2000  rpm in the mixing mode and $2~\mathrm{min}$ at $2200~\mathrm{rpm}$ in the defoaming mode. A uniformly mixed compound was thermally dried at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to evaporate the residual solvents of DMF and MEK in TPU. Finally, the solvent‐dried MOF derivatives/TPU compound was compressed at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $20\mathrm{MPa}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to fabricate the electromagnetic wave absorption composites. During the hot‐pressing process, a vacuum‐assisted system with a thickness gauge of $500\upmu\mathrm{m}$ was applied to produce designed composites with desired thicknesses and devoid of air voids and pores.
## 2.4  Characterization
Field‐emission scanning electron microscopy (FE‐SEM) images were obtained using a JEOL JSM‐6700F field‐ emission scanning electron microscope. Powder X‐ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained using a Rigaku
Ultima IV equipped with a graphite‐monochromatic $\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha$ radiation source $(40\ \mathrm{kV},\ 100\ \mathrm{mA})$ . The magnetic properties were measured using a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM). The high‐resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) images were obtained using an FEL Titan G2 ChemiSTEM Cs probe. The nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were recorded using BELSORP MAX volumetric adsorption equipment and measured via the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method after pretreatment under dynamic vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ . Moreover, thermalgravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed using a TGA55 instrument, and X‐ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted using a Thermo Scientific NEXSA XPS system with a monochromatic $\mathrm{Al-K}\alpha$ radiation source, calibrated based on the standard C 1s peak $(284.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ . Mechanical properties were measured by a universal testing machine (UTM, Instron 3344, Instron) with a cross‐head speed of $2~\mathrm{{mm/min}}$ . Based on the recorded stress and strain, the tensile strength, elongation, and Young’s modulus were investigated. Specifically, the electromagnetic parameters of the composites were measured by a vector network analyzer (VNA, N5222B, Keysight) with $X^{-}$ , Ku‐, and K‐band rectangular waveguides (WR‐90, WR‐62, WR‐42, Keysight) in the frequency range of 8.2–12.4, 12.4–18.0, and $18.0{-}26.5~\mathrm{GHz}$ , respectively. Finally, the samples were obtained by processing the composites into rectangular dimensions of $22.86~\mathrm{mm}\times10.16~\mathrm{mm}$ , $15.80~\mathrm{mm}\times7.90~\mathrm{mm}$ , and $10.67~\mathrm{mm}\times4.32~\mathrm{mm}$ for the measurement with the integration of $X^{-}$ , Ku‐, and K‐band waveguides in VNA, respectively.
## 3  Results and discussion
The synthesis of the $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ bimetallic MOF and the subsequent fabrication process of the MOF‐derived $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ , denoted as MO and MC, respectively, is illustrated in Fig. 1a. A solvothermal method with slight modifications to the reported Fe‐MIL‐88A synthesis method [30] was followed to successfully synthesize the FeCo‐MOF precursor using $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , and fumaric acid. From the FE‐ SEM images in Fig. 1b, the synthesized FeCo‐MOF appears diamond‐shaped and regular in size with an average length of $6\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a width of $3.7\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . After calcination under air for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the FeCo‐MOF transformed into MO and exhibited shrinkage (Fig. 1c) owing to the oxidation of the metal ions and the decomposition of the fumarates during the heat treatment [32]. In contrast, the FeCo‐MOF was converted into MC via pyrolysis under Ar for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ (Fig.  1d). After high‐temperature heat treatment, both MO and MC maintained the diamond‐shaped morphology of the FeCo‐MOF, despite a slight reduction in its size to an average length of $4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and a width of $2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The uniform size of the MOF derivatives enabled a homogeneous distribution without agglomeration, resulting in consistent permittivity. This feature is advantageous for predicting and controlling the electromagnetic wave absorption properties.
Furthermore, we performed TEM analysis to investigate the inner structure and elemental distribution of MO and MC. The TEM images of MO show diamond‐shaped structures as depicted in Fig. 1e. The HR‐TEM image exhibited in Fig. 1f indicates that MO has completely transformed into $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with a lattice spacing of $0.25\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to the (311) plane of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ . The elemental mapping images presented in Fig. $1\mathrm{g}$ indicates that the individual elements of Fe, $\mathbf{\mathrm{Co}}$ , and O were homogeneously dispersed in MO. Figure 1h shows the presence of particle‐embedded porous carbon, which is similar to that observed in MC. This indicates the migration of the free electrons within MC that contributes to the conductive loss because of the interconnected electron‐transport carbon network [33]. As depicted in Fig. 1i, the lattice spacings of $0.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $0.35~\mathrm{nm}$ correspond to the (110) plane of FeCo and the (002) plane of graphitic‐like carbon, respectively, indicating the transformation of MC to form a structure with FeCo embedded in porous carbon. The elemental mapping shown in Fig. 1j demonstrates the uniform distribution of Fe, Co, and C elements throughout the entire MC structure.
Furthermore, the thermal decomposition of the FeCo‐ MOF was analyzed using TGA to determine the optimal heat‐treatment temperature for the formation of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ . The weight loss of the FeCo‐MOF measured in air and Ar is depicted in Fig. S1, demonstrating that the FeCo‐MOF is fully transformed to ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ after calcination at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air. As the heat‐treatment temperature increased, the magnetic properties of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ materials improved, and the highest saturation magnetization was achieved at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [33], which was an appropriate calcination temperature for $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ formation with a yield of $36\%$ . Under Ar atmosphere, the weight loss gradually decreased from 200 to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ owing to the decomposition of fumaric acid in the FeCo‐MOF [34]. Notably, a higher pyrolysis temperature results in a higher crystallinity, which in turn leads to improved saturation magnetization and coercivity [35]. Although a high temperature could enhance the magnetic properties of the materials, the yield decreased continuously after $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Therefore, the optimal pyrolysis temperature in this study was determined as $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with an appropriate yield of $34\%$ for the formation of $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ .
The XRD patterns of the FeCo‐MOF displayed in Fig. 2a vary slightly from the simulated pattern of Fe‐MIL‐88A. Specifically, the peaks assigned to the (100) and (002) planes shifted toward lower and higher angles, respectively. This deviation is attributed to the flexible behavior of the MIL‐88 structure—known as the breathing effect—as well as the substitution of the Fe ions with Co ions [36]. After the calcination of the FeCo‐MOF, the pattern of MO matched with that of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (ICSD 192790), confirming the successful conversion of the FeCo‐MOF precursor into $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 2b). Simultaneously, the pattern of MC matched with that of FeCo (ICSD 155839) after pyrolysis (Fig. 2c). The broad peak at approximately $23.5^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (002) lattice plane of the microporous structure of amorphous carbon [37]. In addition, TGA was performed to estimate the carbon content of MC (Fig. S2). MC was calcined under an air atmosphere, revealing a weight increase at around 250 $\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ attributed to the oxidation of $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ particles. Subsequently, the carbon underwent combustion to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , indicating the combustion of the carbon component around 420 $\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ . The carbon content in MC can be inferred $8\mathrm{\mt}\%$ . As a result, the FeCo‐MOF could be completely transformed into $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ by controlling the heat‐treatment conditions.
As depicted in Fig. 2d ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were obtained to confirm the porosity of the samples, and the surface area was measured using the BET method. The as‐synthesized FeCo‐MOF exhibited a BET surface area of $56~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and total pore volume of 0.11 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . MO exhibited a non‐porous structure owing to the high‐temperature calcination in air, yielding a type‐II isotherm with a small BET surface area of $1.3~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and total pore volume of $0.004~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . In contrast, MC displayed a typical type‐IV isotherm with a micro‐ and mesoporous structure with a large BET surface area of $102{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}}$ and total pore volume of $0.15~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . Owing to carbon layers generated during pyrolysis, MC exhibited a significantly larger BET surface area than other samples analyzed in this study. Based on the non‐local density functional theory (NLDFT), the pore volume of MC (Fig. 2e) was calculated as $0.17\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . MC consists of micropores with widths of 0.56 and $1.16~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and a broad distribution profile of mesopores, indicating a hierarchical porous structure. The presence of micropores can be associated with the pore sizes of their inherent MIL-88A structure [38], while the pyrolysis process of the FeCo‐MOF in Ar at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ likely facilitated the formation of mesopores of various sizes owing to the carbonization of fumaric acid, resulting in the development of a hierarchical porous structure [39]. This hierarchical porous structure possesses abundant interfaces that induce multi‐polarization relaxation loss, which is advantageous for dielectric and conductive loss pathways, extending the attenuation capacity [40]. In addition, it may give rise to multiple reflections of incident waves, increasing the scattering loss [41].
In general, materials with higher saturation magnetization $(M_{\mathrm{s}})$ and coercivity $(H_{\mathrm{c}})$ exhibit stronger electromagnetic wave absorption in the GHz frequency when compared with nonmagnetic materials. This is because high $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ values result in large complex permeability, which can be beyond the Snoek’s limitation [42]. The magnetic properties of the FeCo‐MOF, MO, and MC were measured using a VSM at a maximum applied field strength of $25{,}000\mathrm{ce}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2f). In particular, MO exhibited an $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ of $82\mathrm{\emug}$ and $H_{\mathrm{c}}$ of 681 Oe, which are proximate to those of bulk ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ [43]. MC represented a ferromagnetic hysteresis loop with an $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ of $177\mathrm{\emu\mathrm{g}}$ and $H_{\mathrm{c}}$ of 294 Oe. Moreover, the $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ of MC exhibited approximately $72\%$ of that of the bulk FeCo $(245\mathrm{\emu/g)}$ [44]. This reduction in $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ can be attributed to the carbon content formed during the carbonization process, which shows nonmagnetic properties. Despite the degradation of the $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ , the presence of carbon enhanced the electrical conductivity, leading to a large attenuation constant and conductive loss, thereby enhancing the electromagnetic wave absorption performances.
The elemental composition and chemical state of the FeCo‐MOF, MO, and MC were investigated via XPS. The survey spectra in Fig. S3a–c reveals the presence of Fe, Co, C, and O. Notably, the amount of O 1s content in MO was higher than that in the FeCo‐MOF owing to the M–O $\mathrm{{\bf{M}=F e}}$ , Co) bond of the lattice oxygen $(529.7\mathrm{eV})$ ) in $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and the presence of oxygen vacancy $(531.4\mathrm{eV})$ , which could induce strong dipole polarization (Fig. S3e) [45]. Moreover, the peak of the C 1s element in MC was enhanced in comparison to that of the FeCo‐MOF owing to the carbon layer generated on the surface during pyrolysis (Fig. S3i). The carbon layer provided domains for space‐ charge polarization, contributing to the enhancement of multiple dielectric loss [24]. The Fe $2p$ spectrum of the FeCo‐ MOF was deconvoluted into two characteristic peaks of the Fe $2p_{3/2}$ peak at 710.8 and the Fe $2p_{1/2}$ peak at $724.3\mathrm{~eV}$ , resulting from a spin‐orbit coupling of $13.5\mathrm{~eV}$ (Fig. 3a) [46]. Additionally, the Fe $2p$ spectrum of MO indicated the presence of $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}\left(709.9e V\right)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ (711.7 and $724.3\mathrm{eV},$ with satellite peaks (717.5 and $730.7\mathrm{eV}$ ) of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ , suggesting the occurrence of a redox reaction involving the Fe ion during calcination (Fig. 3b) [47]. The Fe $2p$ spectrum of MC exhibited two major peaks at 707.1 and $720.2\mathrm{eV}.$ corresponding to $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ and indicating the formation of metallic iron in the FeCo bimetallic system of MC (Fig. 3c) [48]. As depicted in Fig. 3d, the $\mathrm{Co}2p$ spectrum of the FeCo‐MOF exhibits Co $2p_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Co}2p_{1/2}$ peaks at 780.8 and $796.6\mathrm{eV}$ respectively, with a deviation of $15.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , indicating the presence of mixed‐valent ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ [49]. Interestingly, the Co $2p$ spectrum of MO exhibited dominant ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ peaks (779.7 and $795.5\mathrm{eV}.$ ) instead of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ peak $(781.8\mathrm{eV})$ , owing to the oxidation of the FeCo‐MOF into $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during calcination (Fig. 3e) [50]. Owing to the formation of FeCo particles in MC through pyrolysis, the peaks of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ (778.4 and $793.5\mathrm{eV},$ ) were prominent in the $\mathrm{Co}2p$ spectrum of MC (Fig. 3f) [50]. Overall, these results indicated the occurrence of a redox reaction in the FeCo‐MOF, forming $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ derivatives via calcination and pyrolysis, respectively.
The electromagnetic wave absorbers were prepared by mixing the FeCo‐MOF derivatives with TPU for the measurement of complex permittivity and permeability (Fig. S4). The critical parameters of electromagnetic wave absorption include relative complex permittivity $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{r}}}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and relative complex permeability $(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}{=}\mu^{\prime}{-}j\mu^{\prime\prime})$ . In general, the real components, $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ , represent the storage capacity, whereas the imaginary components, $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ , represent the attenuation capacity for the electric and magnetic energy of the incident electromagnetic wave, respectively [51]. The dielectric and magnetic tangent losses, expressed as $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mathrm{g}}{=}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\tan\delta_{\upmu}=\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime}$ , indicate the dielectric and magnetic losses ability of the absorbers, respectively, for the electromagnetic waves [13]. The fundamental parameters for the electromagnetic wave absorbers of MO and MC are depicted in Fig. S5. As the frequency increases, the complex permittivity decreases progressively because of the lagging dipole polarization under the application of an electromagnetic field, which is known as the frequency dispersion effect [15, 52]. The $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ of the MC composite exhibited a large value in comparison with that of MO in the $X^{-}$ ${\mathrm{Ku}}^{-}$ , and K‐bands of the entire frequency, accounting for the high polarization and electrical conductivity resulting from numerous defects and interfaces of carbon layer on the surface of pyrolysis‐generated MC. Specifically, the value of $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ is significantly high and directly proportional to the electric conductivity $(\sigma)$ , which is defined as $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}{=}\sigma/2\pi f\varepsilon_{0}$ , where $f$ denotes the frequency and $\varepsilon_{0}$ indicates the permittivity in a vacuum based on the free electron theory [27, 53], displaying a high electrical conductivity because of the carbon layer. The dielectric tangent loss of MC is notably higher than that of MO, which can be ascribed to the formation of the carbon layer, in contrast to presence of an oxide layer on MO, exhibiting a low electrical conductivity. Furthermore, the micropores with the corresponding large specific surface area of MC for space‐charge polarization under charge accumulation at the interfaces effectively enhanced the dielectric loss [37]. The complex permeability of MC is relatively higher than that of MO, which can be interpreted by Globus equation: $\mu{=}(M_{\mathrm{s}}{}^{2}D/K_{1})^{12}$ , where $D$ denotes the average grain size, and $K_{1}$ represents the magnetocrystalline anisotropy constant [16]. Based on this equation, a higher $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ yields a higher complex permeability, which is consistent with the results of VSM measurements in Fig. 2f. The $\mu^{\prime}$ values exhibited a downward trend with increasing frequency, potentially because of the relaxation process of the magnetic moment [27]. Moreover, the curve of the real component $\mu^{\prime}$ of MC displayed a steep slope because of the soft magnetism caused by its lower coercivity than MO, and a broad resonant peak was detected on the curve of $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of MC owing to the eddy current loss, natural resonance, and exchange resonance in the X‐band frequency [54]. The $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\upmu}$ was lower than $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{g}}$ , which confirmed the dominant role of the dielectric loss in electromagnetic wave attenuation.
Across the entire frequency range, the complex permittivity of MO and MC was considerably low and high, respectively, to satisfy the impedance matching and minimize the reflection of the incident electromagnetic wave [55]. Therefore, harmonious impedance matching characteristics could be accomplished by manipulating the complex permittivity value within the range of each parameter using the rule of mixtures for desirable permittivity and modulating the concentration of MO and MC via hybridization. The hybridized absorbers of MO and MC were designated as $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}},$ where $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ and y represent the concentration of MO and MC, respectively, and their mechanical properties were examined. As illustrated in Fig. S6a, TPU underwent complete combustion above $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while $\mathrm{MO}_{75}\mathrm{MC}_{25}$ decreased by $30\mathrm{\wt\%}$ owing to the 70:30 mixture of MOF derivatives and TPU. The tensile strength is observed to be lower value of 6.62, 6.87, and $6.09\mathrm{MPa}$ for $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ , respectively, compared to the pristine TPU $\mathrm{19.61\MPa}$ ). The elongation of $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ $(316\%)$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ $(337\%)$ , and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ $(324\%)$ was decreased compared to the pristine TPU $(500\%)$ . Young's modulus showed the same tendency as elongation (Fig. S6b, c). Overall, the mechanical properties of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ exhibited similar values despite different concentration ratios of
MO and MC. The complex permittivity and permeability of $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ are displayed in Fig. 4. The electromagnetic parameters of the $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ absorbers ranged between those of MO and MC, depending on the concentration ratio. The complex permittivity of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ absorbers ranged from 15.3–12.8, 11.9–10.5, and 9.7–8.9 in terms of the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ , and 7.1–3.5, 3.3–1.8, and 1.9–1.2 in terms of the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ of $\mathrm{MO}_{70}\mathrm{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ , respectively, thereby demonstrating a frequency dispersion behavior identical to that suggested by the complex permittivity of MO and MC. Furthermore, the complex permeability of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ demonstrated a range of 1.29–1.03 and 0.22–0.11 for $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ , respectively. As the concentration of MO with inherently weak electromagnetic properties increased in the $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ absorber, the electromagnetic parameters displayed decreasing trend across the entire frequency. As demonstrated in Fig. S7 and Table S1, the $M_{\mathrm{s}}$ values of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ exhibits a similar trend of gradual reduction. Moreover, $\mu^{\prime}$ was apparently inversed at ${\sim}21\mathrm{GHz}$ frequency owing to the precipitous slope of the $\mu^{\prime}$ curve of MC, whereas the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ curve of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ exhibited a slightly broad resonant peak in the $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ ‐band frequency owing to the hybridization with MC.
In detail, the electromagnetic loss properties can be investigated based on the complex permittivity and permeability for deriving insight related to the electromagnetic wave absorption performance. Based on the Debye theory, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{r}}},\varepsilon^{\prime}$ , and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ can be described as follows [6]:
$$
\varepsilon_{r}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+j2\pi f\tau};
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+(2\pi f)^{2}\tau^{2}};
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}=\frac{2\pi f\tau\left(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}\right)}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}},
$$
where $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}$ denotes the static permittivity; $\varepsilon_{\infty}$ indicates the permittivity at high frequencies; and $\tau$ represents the polarization relaxation time. The curve of $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon"$ can be extrapolated using the above Eqs. (1)–(3) in the form of a Cole–Cole semicircle with $[(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}+\varepsilon_{\infty})/2,0]$ as the center and $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}-\varepsilon_{\infty})/2$ as the radius, as expressed as follows [52]:
$$
\left({\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}.
$$
Each semicircle corresponds to a single polarization relaxation loss, and as illustrated in Fig. 5, the upward tail thoroughly commensurate with the conductive loss [25, 56]. Specifically, numerous small and irregular semicircles were observed on the dominant upward tail of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ (Fig. S8), indicating the existence of conductive loss accompanied by small multipolarization relaxation processes under the application of an alternating electromagnetic field [53]. The abundant nanosized defects and interfaces acting as centers of dipole polarization and space‐charge polarization resulted in the multipolarization Debye relaxation loss [3, 26]. The Cole–Cole semicircle curves of MO and MC, as the predecessors of the hybridized form of $\mathrm{{MO}_{\mathrm{{x}}}\mathrm{{MC}_{\mathrm{{y}}},}}$ , are plotted in Fig. S9. Although the superimposed semicircles were observed on the curve of MO in the $X^{-}$ , Ku‐, and K‐ bands across the entire frequency, the absolute scale of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ was inadequately small to influence the polarization behavior [16]. However, the numerous small deformed semicircles and the upward tail of MC as observed in the curves indicated a consequential contribution to the polarization relaxation loss and conductive loss for $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ owing to the introduction of the carbon layer.
Moreover, the eddy current loss coefficient $(C_{0})$ can be defined as follows [37]:
$$
C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}=\frac23\pi\mu_{0}d^{2}\sigma
$$
where $\mu_{0}$ denotes the permeability in a vacuum, and $d$ indicates the thickness. $C_{0}$ would be constant throughout the measured frequency when eddy current loss is the main effect on magnetic loss [2]. The imaginary part of permittivity needs to be escalating, while the real part of permittivity needs to be descending as the frequency increases for the constant value of $C_{0}$ . The curves of $C_{0}$ indicate a plateau in the initial frequency range confirming that the magnetic loss was predominantly accounted by the eddy current loss owing to the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ demonstrating the increasing and declining tendency, respectively, in the initial frequency range, as shown in Fig. 4d, e. After the middle frequency range, downward trend is observed, as shown in Fig. 6a, indicating various magnetic losses, such as natural resonance and exchange resonance. Conventionally, magnetic hysteresis loss, domain wall resonance, eddy current loss, natural resonance, and exchange resonance denote the key components of the magnetic loss. However, the presence of a high alternating magnetic field is inevitable for producing the magnetic hysteresis loss, and the domain wall resonance generally occurs at a low frequency [57]. Therefore, the primary sources of the magnetic loss include the eddy current loss, natural resonance, and exchange resonance.
The total attenuation abilities of the absorbers were evaluated by the attenuation constant $(\alpha)$ using the following equation [56]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}+\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}},}
$$
where $c$ denotes the speed of light in free space; $\alpha$ signifies the attenuation capacity against the energy of the incident electromagnetic wave propagating through the absorbers. Therefore, $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ demonstrates higher $\alpha$ values for higher MC contents owing to the enhanced dielectric and magnetic losses, exhibiting a trend analogous to that of complex permittivity and permeability, as depicted in Fig.  6b. In this study, the predominant losses of dipole polarization, space‐charge polarization, and conductive loss for dielectric and magnetic losses with the staple resonances significantly dissipated the energy of the electromagnetic wave to effectively enhance the electromagnetic wave absorption.
Electromagnetic wave absorption performances of $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , and $\mathrm{MO}_{80}\mathrm{MC}_{20}$ were assessed by the reflection loss calculated using the following equations based on the transmission line theory [8]:
$$
R L=20l o g\bigg|\frac{Z_{i n}-Z_{0}}{Z_{i n}+Z_{0}}\bigg|;
$$
$$
Z_{i n}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}t a n h\left[j\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}\right];
$$
$$
Z_{0}={\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{0}}{\varepsilon_{0}}}},
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ denotes the input impedance, and $Z_{0}$ indicates the impedance in free space. The reflection loss was strongly correlated with the complex permittivity, permeability, and thickness in the measured frequency range. In Fig. 7, the minimum reflection loss is $-23.24~\mathrm{dB}$ at $25.96\:\mathrm{GHz}$ and $0.8~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness, $-52.29~\mathrm{dB}$ at $10.78\ \mathrm{GHz}$ and $1.9~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness, and − 16.15 dB at $9.42\mathrm{GHz}$ and $2.4\mathrm{mm}$ thickness for $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ , respectively. Owing to the lower complex permittivity and permeability, the matching frequency corresponding to the minimum reflection loss tends to shift toward the lower frequency, as indicated in the two‐dimensional plot [13]. Moreover, as illustrated in the three‐dimensional and contour plot, the thickness corresponding to the minimum reflection loss increases continuously. The effective bandwidth below − 10 dB, signifying $90\%$ absorption, increased up to $7.23~\mathrm{GHz}$ $(19.24-26.47~\mathrm{GHz})$ at $0.9\ \mathrm{mm}$ thickness, $6.16~\mathrm{GHz}$ (19.69–25.85 GHz) at $1.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ thickness, and $3.25~\mathrm{GHz}$ (12.40–15.65  GHz) at $1.7\ \mathrm{mm}$ thickness for $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ , $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , and $\mathbf{MO}_{80}\mathbf{MC}_{20}$ , respectively. We compared the effective absorption bandwidth versus thickness for the representative MOF‐derived absorbers published recently to highlight the electromagnetic wave absorption properties of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ as shown in Fig. S10 (details in Table S2). Furthermore, the electromagnetic wave absorption of MO and MC deteriorates conspicuously, as shown in Fig. S11, which can be ascribed to the excessively low and high complex permittivity, respectively, deteriorating the impedance matching characteristics (described as normalized input impedance of ${Z=}|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|)$ , as depicted in Fig. S12. In addition to the dielectric loss, magnetic loss, and attenuation constant, the impedance matching is a key factor for enhancing the absorption performance. The electromagnetic wave that completely transmits through the absorber without reflection is ideally realized when $Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}$ $(Z=1)$ ) [5, 58]. The $Z$ value of MO and MC demonstrates that the impedance matching substantially exceeds $Z=1$ , which is represented by the gray dashed line, suggesting the reflection of the incident electromagnetic wave at the absorber surface owing to the impedance mismatch, which eventually results in low absorption ability.
Moreover, the electromagnetic wave absorption behavior was confirmed based on the geometrical effect of the quarter‐wavelength matching model, signifying that the two waves reflected from the absorber surface and from the interface between the absorber and metallic background were out of phase by $180^{\circ}$ . In addition, the thickness was equal to the odd multiple of the quarter wavelength, causing the destruction of the waves and resulting in the high‐ efficiency electromagnetic wave absorption. Based on this model, the theoretical matching thickness $(t_{\mathrm{m}})$ was evaluated by the following equation [58]:
$$
t m={\frac{n\lambda}{4}}={\frac{n c}{4f m\sqrt{\left|\varepsilon r\right|\left|\mu r\right|}}}\quad(\mathrm{n}=1,3,5,\ldots),
$$
where $\lambda$ denotes the electromagnetic wave wavelength within the absorber, and $f_{\mathrm{m}}$ indicates the peak frequency. As observed in Fig. 7, $t_{\mathrm{m}}$ is inversely proportional to $f_{\mathrm{m}}$ , and the reflection loss peak shifts toward low frequencies with increasing thickness [7]. The matching thickness on the corresponding $f_{\mathrm{m}}(t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\mathrm{exp}}})$ under the condition of $\lambda4$ , and the normalized input impedance of $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ are illustrated in Fig. 8, which represents the promising applications of $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ because of its minimum reflection loss and the reasonably broad effective bandwidth with a thin matching thickness $<2.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, $t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~exp~}}$ was located on the $\ensuremath{\lambda}\ensuremath{\vert4\vert}$ curve of $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , which was consistent with the quarter‐wavelength matching model and confirmed the realization of the optimum reflection loss when the thickness was in accordance with Eq. (10). Consequently, the model was regarded as significant to design the appropriate thickness of the electromagnetic wave absorber with measured $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{r}}$ . In particular, the $Z$ value approximated to 1 in the X‐band frequency, demonstrating the minimal reflection loss in the corresponding frequency region, which could be attributed to the excellent impedance matching with a suitable matching thickness [28]. In addition, compared to $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , the rest of the analyzed $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ absorbers conformed to the quarter‐wavelength matching model, despite representing a comparatively low absorption performance due to the weaker impedance matching characteristics, as displayed in
A schematic of a feasible mechanism is proposed in Fig. 9 to exemplify the electromagnetic wave absorption based on the analysis and results obtained for the $\mathbf{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{MC}_{\mathrm{y}}$ absorbers. First, the incident electromagnetic waves easily penetrated the absorber without any reflection from the interface between the air and absorber by the appropriate impedance matching. Second, the electromagnetic absorption performances were primarily improved by the multiple loss mechanisms of dielectric and magnetic losses with a high attenuation constant, which can convert electromagnetic waves into thermal energy [59]. The numerous micropores acting as defects and abundant heterointerfaces between MO, MC, carbon, and oxide layers with large specific surface areas indispensably affected the high relaxation loss, which is produced by a strong dipole polarization and space‐charge polarization of asymmetric charge distribution [60]. In particular, the interconnected conductive network of MC, as a carbon source, with long dissipative transmission pathway for electrons migration attenuated the electromagnetic energy, which was beneficial for improving the conductive loss [32]. Also, the porous structure of MC with a large surface area may cause multiple reflections of incident waves to enhance the scattering loss [41]. In addition, the MOF intrinsically introduced with Fe and Co bimetallic elements exhibited a high magnetic loss in the GHz frequency of $X^{-}$ , Ku‐, and K‐ bands, which originated from the eddy current loss, natural resonance, and exchange resonance, and ultimately, aided the excellent dissipation of the electromagnetic waves. Third, because of the corresponding quarter‐wavelength matching thickness, the reflected waves interacted destructively to obtain a minimal reflection loss. Furthermore, owing to the enhancement of the dielectric and magnetic losses with the corresponding appropriate impedance matching at the quarter‐wavelength matching thickness, the reflected waves could be effectively dissipated, thereby affording a high and broadband absorption performance.
## 4  Conclusions
In summary, this study utilized the MIL‐88A‐structured FeCo‐MOF as a precursor to synthesize $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{FeCo/C}$ via calcination and pyrolysis, respectively, for fabricating an excellent electromagnetic wave absorber. The MO and MC possessed diamond‐shaped morphology with good magnetic properties, such as saturation magnetization and relative complex permeability, owing to the homogeneous Fe/Co bimetallic system. In particular, the microporous MC with a large specific surface area induced multi‐relaxation loss and conductive loss to achieve enhanced attenuation capacity. Moreover, MO strongly influenced the mediation of inordinate dielectric properties to satisfy the optimized impedance matching characteristics. The synergistic effects of the hybridization of MO and MC by the rule of mixtures, which is advantageous for controlling the electromagnetic properties because of a uniform size distribution, resulted in improved dielectric and magnetic losses, satisfying the requisites of impedance and quarter‐wavelength matching. In particular, the absorber with an MO:MC concentration ratio of 75:25, i.e., $\mathbf{MO}_{75}\mathbf{MC}_{25}$ , demonstrated the highest electromagnetic wave absorption ability, with a minimum reflection loss of $-52.29~\mathrm{dB}$ at $10.78~\mathrm{GHz}$ and a thickness of $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ . Furthermore, the effective bandwidth was $7.23\mathrm{GHz}$ (19.24–26.47 GHz) below $-10\mathrm{dB}$ for $\mathbf{MO}_{70}\mathbf{MC}_{30}$ at a thickness of $0.9\mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, the study findings suggest that the hybridization of MOF derivatives can serve as a promising approach for developing high‐potential electromagnetic wave absorbers with high reflection loss and broad effective bandwidth at low thicknesses.
